From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:29:56 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 38/38] client: Minecraft's music from the user's music folder

A MusicPlayer plays the tracks of <path_user>/music/<set>/*.ogg as
Minecraft's MusicManager plays its music: one at a time, the first 5 s
after the program starts (its 100 ticks), then a random pause of 10 to
20 minutes in a game (12000 to 24000 ticks) and of 1 to 30 s on the
menu (20 to 600 ticks), never the same track twice in a row, at
music_volume (a setting of its own, as Minecraft's slider, on top of
sound_volume). The set is "menu" on the main menu, "creative" (which
also plays "game") with the creative privilege, "water" with the camera
in a liquid and "game" otherwise, as Minecraft picks its creative and
under water music. The delay outlives the menu and the games, as
Minecraft's does, and a situation with a shorter longest pause cuts it
down, so a game's track comes soon after the menu's; a track cut short
by the end of its sound manager (leaving a game, entering one) is
followed within half a second, as Minecraft's menu music replaces the
game's.

The tracks are streamed from the file (loadSoundFile) and never go
through the server's media: Minecraft's 66 game tracks are 250 MB, and
the client keeps every media file in memory on a console with 512 MB.
The end of a track is known from its length (ov_time_total) rather than
from the sound manager's removed sounds, which the client's own
bookkeeping consumes. Nothing plays without the folder, and nothing
ships with the engine.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>

